                            "id": "review-f778fb6669cc1e1190ff29e613430e28",
                            "question": "Refine this freshly generated plan before any implementation is run. The user authorized repeated planning from VISION.md and quality improvements, while requiring a task DAG at least as good as the backed-up plan. This is a planning-quality assessment, not numerical evidence or approval of a solver.\n\nThe task/artifact model is coherent, but two related work-package/sequencing weaknesses remain:\n1. T-passive-coupling implements the first two-way foundation and performs focused checks, yet it has no separate bounded passive qualification campaign or review gate. T-transfers and T-actuators can be implemented before that foundation is accepted; the only two-way adoption campaign, T-adoption, happens after constructing the whole active prototype. Preserve the backed-up plan's useful early passive feasibility decision by inserting a bounded passive campaign after T-passive-coupling and before substantial dependent resource/activity work. Cover relevant physical references, repeated cycles/refinement, local error, rollback and complete coupled cost using already approved budgets. Keep negative/inconclusive findings distinct from completion and retain T-adoption's later active proof plus the separate population review. This is not a request to invent results or new numerical tolerances.\n2. T-field currently combines stationary fluid implementation and species transport; make their independently testable work explicit. Establish stationary flow before prescribed-boundary/two-way passive work, then establish conservative species transport against the qualified moving/passive foundation before transfers and actuation. Keep their design prerequisites sufficient and don't make stationary moving-boundary tests require an as-yet-unimplemented full species solver. A bounded tracer/reference probe can be scoped explicitly if needed for a moving-geometry test.\n\nMake the smallest coherent plan edits that resolve these weaknesses. Preserve the successful bootstrap/supervisor separation, design reconciliation and distinct human approval, core ownership, deferred broader scope and other unaffected tasks. Preserve stable IDs for continuing work. Update affected task contexts, criteria, dependencies, artifact descriptions/references, and the gate decision together. Keep new design/implementation/evidence artifacts distinct; reuse the same artifact ID for shared consumers. All tasks are unrun, so this request justifies plan edits, not implementation repairs or claims of failed code. Return a concrete propose_changes response if warranted under the existing reviewer schema.",
